From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Quarrier <squarrier@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 16:49:53 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] glm5_next/cuda: prefill lanes (TF_GLM_PREFILL_LANES) and FP8
 wire rows (TF_GLM_WIRE_FP8), glm53f-afd's prefill pipeline on the AFD split

Hugh Madden (@dangerm00se, github.com/hughmadden) authored the two pieces of glm53f-afd's prefill pipeline that this
brings to the attention/expert split, both behind switches that are off by default: the prefill in up to four lanes
(the two-lane prefill of whole prompt chunks in mimo26f-afd @ bab9fa2, crates/mimo26-coordinator/src/dforward.rs;
four lanes of 2,048 rows by default in glm53f-afd @ 91db3cc, crates/glm53f-forward/src/forward.rs run_lanes,
src/experts.rs ExpertBackend::depth) and the FP8 E4M3 wire rows with a UE8M0 scale per 32 values (Fp8E4m3Ue8m0K32:
glm53f-afd @ 91db3cc crates/glm53f-coordinator/kernels/wire.cu, src/wire.rs, src/fp8.rs, from mimo26f-afd's kernels).
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Turquoise Bay AI Pty Ltd. The wire row format is DS41RT's, by T.J. Purtell (@wrldsuksgo2mars,
tpurtell/ds41rt, MIT), carried through mimo26f-afd. No code is copied.

TF_GLM_PREFILL_LANES=N (1 .. 4; unset follows TF_GLM_PREFILL_PAIRS: 2 with it on, else 1):
- prefill_pairs.py: run_lanes runs N consecutive whole prompt chunks through the layers together, the prompt chunk pairs
  generalized. _forward_lanes interleaves their layer loops by three rules: the next exchange goes out in lane order
  when fewer than the wire's depth are in flight; else a lane that needs no reply runs its attention once the lane
  before it ran that layer's; else the oldest exchange is collected. Two lanes make exactly the pairs' schedule
  (run_pair and _forward_pair are run_lanes and _forward_lanes of two; the tests keep the pairs' _forward_pair as the
  oracle). Lanes 1 .. N - 1 of a group of three or more hold their own routed rows (lane_buffers), since a lane can
  wait to send while later lanes run their attention; the reply rows and the shared expert's output stay shared,
  which needs one exchange in flight (depth 1, the MCDMA wire's: a deeper wire is refused). buffers_for picks the
  pair's or the lanes' buffers (a pair in a run of more lanes reuses the first lane's). Held states at every lane's
  end but the last's.
- multi.py: _pair_plan plans up to N chunks, each the next as a pair's second is to its first, with the pairs' rules
  for each (no stream decoding, no sliced chunk suspended, no MTP stream, no replay); a chunk that ends a prompt is
  the last; two chunks run as a pair, three or more as lanes. _fill_pair does each chunk's host work in order as
  for a pair. /health's prefill_lanes counters (groups, chunks, rows) when N >= 3.
- afd.py: MODE_LANES (9), header word 10 the lanes' count; begin_lanes opens N windows (begin_pair's code for N);
  the check names lane groups; TF_AFD_TIME's lane groups get their own line, keyed by N and R.
- afd_mcdma.py / experts_node.py: the attention writes N windows' header tables (word 8 the next lane's rows); the
  expert takes window k's first request after window k - 1's layer-0 reply and serves each layer's lanes in lane
  order, checking each later lane's header against the first's; MODE_LANES only when the settings handshake carried
  "lanes" (an older expert refuses the settings), and no more lanes than it agreed to.
- engine.py: the later lanes' buffers at startup and in the capacity plan (160.8 MiB a lane at 2,048 rows on
  GLM-5.3-Flash with DFlash2's taps, against the pair's 144.2), the handshake's "pairs" and "lanes", the setting read
  before the wire opens.

TF_GLM_WIRE_FP8=1 (changes the experts' inputs, so outputs; gated on KL before use):
- wire_fp8.py: the codec, glm53f-afd's arithmetic: per 32 values the UE8M0 byte clamp(ceil(log2(amax / 448)) + 127,
  0, 254) (0 for zeros, NaN skipped), each value times 2^(127 - s) in fp32, the nearest E4M3 code with ties to the
  larger magnitude, saturating at 448, the sign of the fp32 product; decode e4m3 x 2^(s - 127) rounded once to bf16.
  The scales are torch operations, the codes a table of every bf16 magnitude's code made with reference_code, a
  plain-Python transcription of glm53f-afd's device encoder; 512 rows a pass.
- mcdma.py: Layout(fp8=True): the request's rows section holds the codes, then the scale bytes padded to 256 bytes
  (4,224 bytes a row at hidden 4,096 against 8,192; 8.25 MiB a request at 2,048 rows against 16 MiB).
- afd_mcdma.py: the attention encodes into its own buffers and packs them as the rows' section; the expert decodes
  after each landing into its own bf16 rows and computes with those. afd.py's wire check hashes the rows the experts
  decode (the transport's wire_rows). The handshake's "wire_fp8": an older expert refuses the settings.

With both switches unset nothing changes: the settings, headers, buffers and every call are the ones before; with
TF_GLM_PREFILL_PAIRS=1 alone the pairs run exactly as before.

Tests: tests/test_glm_prefill_lanes.py (the setting, handshake and bytes; two lanes equal the pairs' schedule on
four layer layouts; the lane schedule's rules for 3 and 4 lanes; each lane's recorded kernel calls equal its solo
chunk's over the real forward, one stream and TF_GLM_MULTI_PREFILL's chunks, with held states; lanes over the real
wire and the fake MCDMA pair, CPU- and GPU-driven, followed by a pair and a plain forward; an expert without lanes,
or with fewer, refuses; lanes stopped early; the lane timer line; at world 1 on the fake model, 3 and 4 lanes equal
the pairs and the setting off in replies, cached counts and kept states for prompts of 1 to 5 chunks, with
TF_GLM_SHARED_PREFIX and TF_GLM_MULTI_PREFILL; no lanes while a stream decodes or a sliced chunk is suspended).
tests/test_glm_wire_fp8.py (the setting, handshake and sizes; every code and back, ties, saturation, NaN, zeros, the
scale bytes; rows of random, tied, tiny, huge, zero, NaN and infinite values byte for byte as the reference; the
table on every bf16 magnitude; the decode exact; the error bound; FP8 rows over the real wire for plain forwards, a
pair and lanes, the experts computing with exactly the decoded rows; the wire check with 0 mismatches).
tests/test_glm_multi_afd.py's and tests/test_glm_sliced_fill.py's fakes take lanes too; the pair tests pin
TF_GLM_PREFILL_LANES unset.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (planning and execution), via Hermes

